title: A YAML-parsed -0.0 into JSON dies in the Math::BigFloat carrier assertion
updated: 2026-08-20T05:45:42Z
---

Found while measuring the emitter corpus for karr #86, not caused by it. Present
at 543b59d and unchanged by #86 (the guard is YAML-only and this is JSON).

  perl -MYAML::XS=Load -MFile::SOPS -e \
    "File::SOPS->encrypt(data => Load(qq{x_unencrypted: -0.0\n}), \
                         recipients => [\$pub], format => q{json})"

  -> Math::BigFloat did not render a float leaf at full precision.
     Check for a global Math::BigFloat->accuracy or ->precision setting.

The message is wrong about the cause and there is no global setting involved.
What happens: YAML::XS keeps the source text, so the leaf is a float carrying a
public PV of "-0.0". Format::JSON::_float_roundtrips answers no (Cpanel quotes a
PV-carrying scalar, so the reparse is a str), the leaf goes to _float_carrier,
and Math::BigFloat->new("-0", undef, undef) stringifies as "0" -- so the
carriers own assertion, which exists to catch a truncated decimal, fires on a
sign it cannot carry.

Measured, sops 3.13.3, leaf x_unencrypted, one document per row:

  perl NV -0.0        -> json  -0.0   sops -d exit 0, reads -0    (works)
  YAML-parsed -0.0    -> json  CROAK                              (this)
  perl NV -0.0        -> yaml  -0.0   sops -d exit 0, reads -0    (works)
  YAML-parsed -0.0    -> yaml  -0.0   sops -d exit 0, reads -0    (works)

So it is one cell of four, and the same value written from a bare NV goes
through fine -- the carrier is reached in both cases, which suggests the
assertion, not the carrier, is what differs.

value_to_bytes writes -0 for a negative zero (ADR 0005); the YAML side handles
it by writing -0.0 in _float_carrier, because a bare -0 is an INTEGER to every
YAML reader. JSON has no such problem: -0 is a valid JSON number. Whether the
right answer is to special-case the sign in the JSON carrier, to let the
assertion compare the double rather than the text, or something else, wants a
measurement of what sops does with a JSON -0 either way.

Wire lane. Measured with sops 3.13.3, Crypt::Age 0.002.
landed in 44a4611 (fix + docs/adr/0014 + t/24 section 15 + Changes). The carrier now takes the -0 branch through unpack/pack instead of Math::BigFloat: 6 of 228 emitter-corpus rows move and all 6 were a croak, 6/6 end-to-end documents sops -d exit 0 reading back -0. Measurement note worth keeping: every ARITHMETIC PV strip (0+$v, $v*1, $v*1.0) loses the sign, immediately or on the second call in the same process, because Perl's arithmetic ops set the private IOK on the caller's scalar in place. The assertion caught that version. Left open here: the carrier callback still gets no key path, so its message cannot name the leaf (karr #68's known gap). Spun off: karr #89, the same sign loss inside the #86 guard's _go_float.
